  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "# Bench Coil Characterisation — a real coil, outside any trap\n",
    "\n",
    "**Standalone.** This notebook shares no code with any other. Every driver, every capture\n",
    "routine and every fit is defined here, so it can be copied to a bare board and run on its\n",
    "own. Where logic was proven elsewhere in the project it has been re-implemented rather than\n",
    "imported, and the reasoning is repeated in the comments so nothing has to be looked up.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "## What this is for\n",
    "\n",
    "Every stage from 8 upward has measured a resonator whose answer was **chosen in software**.\n",
    "This one measures a physical coil sitting on the bench — no trap, no ion, no cryostat — where\n",
    "nothing is known in advance.\n",
    "\n",
    "That removes the project's central safeguard, so it is replaced by three others:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Replacement | What it catches |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| **Self-test against the emulator, before and after** | Any fault in the instrument itself. The emulator is a register bit, not a cable, so it can be switched back in at any moment |\n",
    "| **Two independent measurements of the same width** | A fit that is wrong for a reason the fit cannot see — a sloping baseline, a stray tone, compression |\n",
    "| **A signal budget computed *and* measured** | Coupling, loading and level problems, which on a bench look exactly like physics |\n",
    "\n",
    "## The coil as wired\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook assumes the coil already has **0.3 pF coupling capacitors** on its drive and\n",
    "pickup ports. That is deliberately very weak coupling, and it has two consequences that shape\n",
    "everything below:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Good:** at 40 MHz a 0.3 pF capacitor is about 13 kΩ of reactance, so almost nothing is\n",
    "  drawn from the tank. The quality factor you measure should be close to the coil's own.\n",
    "- **Awkward:** the pickup capacitor and whatever capacitance follows it — cable, connector,\n",
    "  board input — form a capacitive divider, and 0.3 pF against even 20 pF of cable is a\n",
    "  division by seventy. **Keep the pickup lead as short as physically possible.** Step 3\n",
    "  measures what you actually got and says whether an amplifier is needed.\n",
    "\n",
    "## What this notebook cannot tell you\n",
    "\n",
    "Anything that needs an ion: the dip, resistive cooling, ion temperature, the phase-sensitive\n",
    "technique, the self-excited oscillator in its real form. Also anything needing electrodes,\n",
    "so nothing from Stage 17. None of that is on the critical path for proving the board works.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Before you run it\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Run Stage 12 first**, while the emulator is still the authority. It calibrates the two\n",
    "   decisions that only work on an emulator.\n",
    "2. Run this notebook **once with the input terminated** (set `COIL_CONNECTED = False`) to\n",
    "   catalogue the board's own stray signals, then again with the coil attached. Searching a\n",
    "   band you have not surveyed is how a stray signal becomes a discovery.\n",
    "3. As root: `sudo jupyter notebook` — everything here reads `/dev/mem`.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Order of the steps\n",
    "\n",
    "| Step | Question | Needs |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| 0 | Is the right bitstream loaded and does everything answer? | — |\n",
    "| 1 | Does the instrument still return a known answer? | emulator |\n",
    "| 2 | Are the levels sane and what else is in the band? | coil or terminator |\n",
    "| 3 | Where is the resonance, and how sharp? | coil |\n",
    "| 4 | How much signal survives the 0.3 pF coupling? | coil |\n",
    "| 5 | Does a second, independent method agree? | coil |\n",
    "| 6 | Is the measurement linear — am I loading or compressing it? | coil |\n",
    "| 7 | Does anything move while I measure? | coil |\n",
    "| 8 | Does feedback change Q and centre as it should? | coil |\n",
    "| 9 | Summary, and the constants later notebooks need | — |"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "# Wiring the coil to the board\n",
    "\n",
    "**Two coaxial runs and one ground. That is the whole rig.** Read the current\n",
    "limit at the bottom before changing either capacitor - it is the only thing\n",
    "standing between a mistyped number and a damaged coil, and it is a piece of\n",
    "physics rather than a piece of software.\n",
    "\n",
    "## The picture\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "                 Red Pitaya STEMlab 125-14\n",
    "      +--------------------------------------------+\n",
    "      |                                            |\n",
    "      |   OUT1  (SMA)                   IN1  (SMA) |\n",
    "      +-----|--------------------------------|-----+\n",
    "            |                                |\n",
    "            |  short coax                    |  short coax\n",
    "            |                                |\n",
    "         C_drive                          C_pick\n",
    "         0.3 pF                           0.3 pF\n",
    "            |                                |\n",
    "            |    +----------------------+    |\n",
    "            +----|   the coil / tank    |----+\n",
    "                 +----------------------+\n",
    "                            |\n",
    "                            |  ONE ground strap, at the board end only\n",
    "                            |\n",
    "                     board ground (SMA shells)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "| Board | Direction | Carries |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **OUT1** (DAC CH A) | out, to the coil | the drive **and** the feedback, already summed |\n",
    "| **IN1** (ADC CH A) | in, from the coil | everything this notebook measures |\n",
    "| **OUT2 / IN2** | - | unused. Leave them empty |\n",
    "| **E1 / E2 headers** | - | **never** for the coil. 3.3 V logic, no protection |\n",
    "\n",
    "## Why only one output cable\n",
    "\n",
    "The feedback loop is closed *inside* the chip, not around the bench. In\n",
    "`red_pitaya_top.sv` the generator and the phase shifter are summed before the\n",
    "converter:\n",
    "\n",
    "```verilog\n",
    "assign dac_a_sum = asg_dat[0] + pid_dat[0];   // generator + feedback\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "`asg_dat[0]` is the swept drive, `pid_dat[0]` is the phase-shifted copy of what\n",
    "came in on IN1. So OUT1 already carries both, and there is no second physical\n",
    "loop to wire. The sum is **saturating**, not wrapping - the line after it clamps\n",
    "to full scale rather than folding through zero - so drive plus feedback can\n",
    "never invert its own sign on the way out.\n",
    "\n",
    "The same wiring serves Step 1's self-test unchanged. The emulator is a\n",
    "multiplexer in front of the phase shifter,\n",
    "\n",
    "```verilog\n",
    "assign adc_dat_ps[0] = reso_ena ? reso_dat : adc_dat[0];\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "so enabling it *replaces* the digitised input. The coil can stay bolted on and\n",
    "cabled; it simply stops being listened to. You never have to unplug anything to\n",
    "get a known answer back.\n",
    "\n",
    "## The two capacitors\n",
    "\n",
    "Both are series, one per port, and both are `C_COUPLE` in the configuration\n",
    "cell. They do three jobs at once:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **They set the signal budget.** 0.3 pF against the ~30 pF of cable, connector\n",
    "   and board input is a divider of about 1/100, or -40 dB. That is what the\n",
    "   notebook's name is about, and what Step 4 predicts and then measures.\n",
    "2. **They block DC absolutely.** The board's outputs are DC-coupled, so this\n",
    "   matters. Belt and braces: the amplitude register `0x04` holds the scale in\n",
    "   bits `[13:0]` and a **DC offset in bits `[29:16]`**, and `Generator.amp()`\n",
    "   writes the whole 32-bit word with the top bits zero - so `set_a_dc` is\n",
    "   pinned at zero on every single write.\n",
    "3. **They limit the current.** See below.\n",
    "\n",
    "## The input jumper\n",
    "\n",
    "Set **LV** on IN1 (±1 V full scale). That is what `V_FULLSCALE = 1.0` in the\n",
    "configuration cell assumes; on HV it would be 20.0 and you would throw away\n",
    "another 26 dB you cannot afford after a 1/100 divider. The input is ~1 MΩ, not\n",
    "50 Ω - `R_IN_BOARD = 1.0e6` - so **do not terminate it**, and keep the coax\n",
    "short. Every extra picofarad of cable lands in `C_STRAY_EST` and costs signal\n",
    "directly. 30 pF is roughly 30 cm of thin coax plus the connector plus the board.\n",
    "\n",
    "## Grounding\n",
    "\n",
    "One strap, at the board end. The two coax braids are already commoned at the SMA\n",
    "shells; if you also tie the coil former to a separate ground you have built a\n",
    "loop around the whole rig, and at 40 MHz it will show up in the Step 2 band\n",
    "survey as structure that moves when you touch the bench. Run Step 2 with\n",
    "`COIL_CONNECTED = False` first and keep the plot - anything that appears in it\n",
    "afterwards is the coil or the room, and a ground loop is the usual answer.\n",
    "\n",
    "## The current limit - the reason this is safe\n",
    "\n",
    "At the top of the search band the pickup capacitor's reactance is\n",
    "\n",
    "| frequency | reactance of 0.3 pF |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| 20 MHz | 26.5 kΩ |\n",
    "| 41 MHz | 12.9 kΩ |\n",
    "| 55 MHz | 9.7 kΩ |\n",
    "\n",
    "OUT1 delivers at most ±1 V into 50 Ω, and `DRIVE_MAX = 0.9` caps it at 0.9 V.\n",
    "Through 12.9 kΩ that is **70 µA peak**, and the power it can deliver into *any*\n",
    "load - matched, mismatched, shorted, open - is bounded by\n",
    "\n",
    "    P_max = V² / (4 X_c) = 0.81 / (4 × 12850) ≈ **16 µW**\n",
    "\n",
    "Sixteen microwatts. There is no setting of `DRIVE_FRAC`, no feedback gain, and\n",
    "no software fault that gets past that, because the limit is the capacitor and\n",
    "not the code. The software guards in this notebook exist to protect the\n",
    "*measurement* - a clipped front end still looks vaguely like a resonance - not\n",
    "to protect the coil. The coil is protected by 0.3 pF of series reactance.\n",
    "\n",
    "> **The one change that removes this protection.** Replace either capacitor with\n",
    "> a direct connection, or with something in the nanofarad range, and OUT1 sees\n",
    "> 50 Ω instead of 13 kΩ: **18 mA and 8 mW**, about 500× more, and a fine-wire\n",
    "> coil can care about that. If you ever do that, recompute `DRIVE_MAX` from the\n",
    "> coil's own dissipation limit *before* the first run, and treat every number in\n",
    "> the configuration cell as untested.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "# Configuration\n",
    "\n",
    "**Everything you might want to change lives in this one cell.** Nothing below it is meant to\n",
    "be edited during normal use. Read the comments before the first run — three of these\n",
    "(`SEARCH_LO`, `SEARCH_HI`, `DRIVE_FRAC`) decide whether Step 4 finds anything at all."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "# ============================ CONFIGURATION ==================================\n",
    "\n",
    "COIL_CONNECTED = True     # False = input terminated. Step 2 becomes a survey of the\n",
    "                          #   board's OWN stray signals, and Steps 3-8 are skipped.\n",
    "                          #   Run it that way ONCE before ever attaching the coil.\n",
    "\n",
    "SOURCE_NOTE    = 'bench coil, 0.3 pF coupling caps both ports, room temperature'\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- where to look for the resonance ---------------------------------------\n",
    "# Generous by default. Narrow it once you know roughly where the coil sits: the\n",
    "# coarse pass costs about one second per megahertz of span.\n",
    "SEARCH_LO      = 20.0e6   # [Hz] bottom of the search band\n",
    "SEARCH_HI      = 55.0e6   # [Hz] top. Must stay below 62.5 MHz (Nyquist)\n",
    "SEARCH_STEP    = 100e3    # [Hz] coarse grid. Must be FINER than the narrowest\n",
    "                          #   linewidth you could plausibly have, or the coarse\n",
    "                          #   pass steps straight over the resonance.\n",
    "                          #   Q = 1000 at 40 MHz is a 40 kHz linewidth -> use 20e3.\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- drive ------------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "DRIVE_FRAC     = 0.9      # generator amplitude, 0..1 of full scale. Start high:\n",
    "                          #   0.3 pF throws most of it away. Step 6 checks that\n",
    "                          #   this is not so high that anything compresses.\n",
    "DRIVE_FRACS_LIN = [0.225, 0.45, 0.9]   # Step 6 linearity ladder (each 6 dB up).\n",
    "                          #   ASCENDING on purpose: the ladder climbs towards the\n",
    "                          #   drive you intend to use rather than starting there.\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- the hard ceiling -------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "DRIVE_MAX      = 0.9      # THE ONLY NUMBER THAT LIMITS WHAT REACHES THE COIL.\n",
    "                          #   Every amplitude write in this notebook is checked\n",
    "                          #   against it and REFUSED if it is larger - there is\n",
    "                          #   no path, mistyped or otherwise, that drives harder\n",
    "                          #   than this. Lower it first and raise it only\n",
    "                          #   deliberately, with the coil in front of you.\n",
    "                          #   Must be in (0, 1.0]: above 1.0 the DAC is asked for\n",
    "                          #   more than full scale and returns a clipped, harmonic\n",
    "                          #   -rich waveform that is no longer a sine.\n",
    "assert 0.0 < DRIVE_MAX <= 1.0, 'DRIVE_MAX must be in (0, 1.0]'\n",
    "assert 0.0 <= DRIVE_FRAC <= DRIVE_MAX, 'DRIVE_FRAC exceeds DRIVE_MAX'\n",
    "assert all(0.0 <= x <= DRIVE_MAX for x in DRIVE_FRACS_LIN), \\\n",
    "    'a value in DRIVE_FRACS_LIN exceeds DRIVE_MAX'\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- receiver ---------------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "DEC_SEARCH     = 8        # log2(R) during the coarse search. R = 256, +-49 kHz\n",
    "                          #   clean band, 488 kHz output rate. Wide and quick.\n",
    "DEC_FIT        = 10       # log2(R) for the careful sweeps. R = 1024, +-12.2 kHz.\n",
    "                          #   Rule: the receiver window should be small next to\n",
    "                          #   the linewidth. Step 4 checks this and complains.\n",
    "N_AVG          = 64       # PEEK register pairs averaged per measurement point\n",
    "SETTLE         = 0.02     # [s] wait after any retune, for the filter pipeline\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- Step 3: signal budget ---------------------------------------------------\n",
    "C_COUPLE       = 0.3e-12  # [F] the coupling capacitors actually fitted\n",
    "C_STRAY_EST    = 30e-12   # [F] your estimate of everything after the pickup cap:\n",
    "                          #   cable + connector + board input. 1 m of RG-58 alone\n",
    "                          #   is about 100 pF. This is only used for a prediction\n",
    "                          #   that the measurement then checks.\n",
    "L_EST          = None     # [H] the coil's inductance, if you know it (e.g. 1e-6).\n",
    "                          #   Optional. With it, Step 4 can also report the coil's\n",
    "                          #   own thermal noise density and how much the pickup\n",
    "                          #   path loads it. Without it those lines are skipped\n",
    "                          #   rather than guessed.\n",
    "R_IN_BOARD     = 1.0e6    # [ohm] the board's input resistance\n",
    "V_FULLSCALE    = 1.0      # [V] input full scale. 1.0 on the LV jumper setting,\n",
    "                          #   20.0 on HV. Used only to print volts instead of\n",
    "                          #   counts - nothing depends on it being right.\n",
    "T_COIL         = 293.0    # [K] coil temperature, for the thermal-noise estimate\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- Step 5: ring-down -------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "RING_DECS      = [8, 64]  # scope decimations tried, in order. The stock scope\n",
    "                          #   honours 1, 8, 64, 1024, ... - other values may be\n",
    "                          #   silently ignored, so stay on that ladder.\n",
    "                          #   dec = 8  -> 1.05 ms record, 64 ns per sample\n",
    "                          #   dec = 64 -> 8.4 ms record, 512 ns per sample\n",
    "                          #   Only dec = 1 is *proven* on this board (Stage 2),\n",
    "                          #   so Step 5 verifies the record length it actually\n",
    "                          #   got rather than assuming.\n",
    "RING_MIN_PTS   = 8        # points per decay time constant below which Step 5\n",
    "                          #   declines to report a number\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- Step 7: drift -----------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "DRIFT_MINUTES  = 10.0     # total observation time\n",
    "DRIFT_PERIOD   = 20.0     # [s] between revisits\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- Step 8: feedback --------------------------------------------------------\n",
    "FB_PHASES      = list(range(0, 360, 20))   # [deg] phase axis\n",
    "FB_GAIN_PROBE  = 0.15     # small gain used to FIND the two special phases safely\n",
    "FB_GAINS       = [0.15, 0.30, 0.45]        # gains for the K-versus-gain check\n",
    "FB_LEVEL_GUARD = 0.80     # abort the point if the input exceeds this fraction of\n",
    "                          #   full scale. THE REAL PATH HAS NO OVERLOAD FLAG - the\n",
    "                          #   emulator's sticky CLIPPED bit has quietly been doing\n",
    "                          #   that job for ten stages and it is gone here.\n",
    "\n",
    "# ---- Step 1: instrument self-test -------------------------------------------\n",
    "SELFTEST_F0    = 37.0e6   # deliberately NOT 40 MHz, so a hard-coded 40 MHz\n",
    "SELFTEST_Q     = 250.0    #   anywhere in the analysis would show up as a failure\n",
    "SELFTEST_TOL_F = 0.20     # tolerance: fraction of a linewidth on the centre\n",
    "SELFTEST_TOL_Q = 0.05     # tolerance: fractional error on Q\n",
    "\n",
    "RESULTS_JSON   = 'bench_coil_results.json'   # written by Step 9\n",
    "\n",
    "FS_CLK         = 125e6    # the one clock everything on the board runs from [Hz]\n",
    "print('configuration loaded. coil connected: %s' % COIL_CONNECTED)\n",
    "print('search band %.1f - %.1f MHz in %.0f kHz steps'\n",
    "      % (SEARCH_LO / 1e6, SEARCH_HI / 1e6, SEARCH_STEP / 1e3))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "# Imports, and the one rule that must not be broken\n",
    "\n",
    "**Every register access is a single 32-bit read or write.** A bulk `mmap` slice becomes a\n",
    "burst transfer that the bridge between the processor's general-purpose port and the internal\n",
    "register bus cannot acknowledge. It locks the bus and reboots the board — no traceback, no\n",
    "log, just a board that vanishes. The loops below look inefficient on purpose; do not\n",
    "\"optimise\" them.\n",
    "\n",
    "Ordinary DDR memory is different. Bulk reads *there* are fine and fast, which is why the\n",
    "capture routine reads its records in one slice while the register drivers never do.\n",
    "\n",
    "The two shims exist because this board carries older numpy and matplotlib than a current\n",
    "desktop: `nan_to_num`'s `nan=` keyword arrived in numpy 1.17 and `Axes.set_facecolor` in\n",
    "matplotlib 2.0."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "import os, mmap, struct, time, json\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "def nan_fill(a, value):\n",
    "    \"\"\"np.nan_to_num(a, nan=value), for numpy versions without the keyword.\"\"\"\n",
    "    out = np.array(a, dtype=float, copy=True)\n",
    "    out[~np.isfinite(out)] = value\n",
    "    return out\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "def set_bg(ax, colour):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Axes background colour, across the matplotlib 1.x -> 2.x rename.\"\"\"\n",
    "    if hasattr(ax, 'set_facecolor'):\n",
    "        ax.set_facecolor(colour)\n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        ax.set_axis_bgcolor(colour)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "def s32(v):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Reinterpret a 32-bit register value as a signed integer.\"\"\"\n",
    "    return v - (1 << 32) if v & 0x80000000 else v\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "def db(x):\n",
    "    \"\"\"Amplitude ratio to decibels, safe at zero.\"\"\"\n",
    "    x = np.asarray(x, dtype=float)\n",
    "    return 20.0 * np.log10(np.maximum(np.abs(x), 1e-300))\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "class Bus(object):\n",
    "    \"\"\"One register block, accessed strictly one 32-bit word at a time.\"\"\"\n",
    "\n",
    "    def __init__(self, base, span=0x1000):\n",
    "        self.base = base\n",
    "        self.fd = os.open('/dev/mem', os.O_RDWR | os.O_SYNC)\n",
    "        self.m = mmap.mmap(self.fd, span, offset=base)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def w(self, off, val):\n",
    "        self.m[off:off + 4] = struct.pack('<I', val & 0xFFFFFFFF)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def r(self, off):\n",
    "        return struct.unpack('<I', self.m[off:off + 4])[0]\n",
    "\n",
    "    def rs(self, off):\n",
    "        return s32(self.r(off))\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "print('numpy %s, matplotlib %s' % (np.__version__, matplotlib.__version__))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "# The four hardware blocks\n",
    "\n",
    "Four register blocks are used. Their full maps live in `acquisition_custom/README.md` and in\n",
    "the header of each Verilog file; only what this notebook needs is repeated here.\n",
    "\n",
    "| Block | Base | Role here |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **Acquisition** | `0x40600000` | The receiver. Used two ways: as a narrowband voltmeter (registers) and as a recorder (memory) |\n",
    "| **Generator** | `0x40200000` | The stimulus. A stored sine replayed at a chosen rate |\n",
    "| **Phase shifter** | `0x40300000` | The feedback controller, used only in Step 8 |\n",
    "| **Digital resonator** | `0x40400000` | The emulator. Used **only** in Step 1, as a known answer, then switched off |\n",
    "| **Scope** | `0x40100000` | Full-rate capture of the raw input. Needed for level checks and the ring-down |\n",
    "\n",
    "## The trick that makes every sweep simple\n",
    "\n",
    "Tune the receiver's local oscillator to **exactly** the drive frequency. The signal then lands\n",
    "at 0 Hz, where the in-phase and quadrature values are steady numbers that can be read from two\n",
    "registers at leisure. At any other offset they rotate, and two register reads land on different\n",
    "samples — an artefact that once convincingly imitated filter droop in this project.\n",
    "\n",
    "\"Exactly\" is doable rather than approximate, and this notebook is stricter about it than\n",
    "earlier stages were. The generator's frequency word has 30 bits; the local oscillator's has\n",
    "32. Both count the *same* 125 MHz clock. So if the generator's word is `step`, the generator\n",
    "is at exactly `step x 125e6 / 2**30`, and setting the local oscillator word to exactly\n",
    "`4 x step` puts it at precisely the same frequency — not to within a rounding error, but\n",
    "identically. `Generator.freq_word()` and `Acq.tune_word()` below make that pairing explicit."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": null,
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "# =============================================================================\n",
    "#  ACQUISITION SYSTEM  -  base 0x40600000\n",
    "# =============================================================================\n",
    "#  Two personalities:\n",
    "#\n",
    "#   * VOLTMETER. Point the local oscillator at the drive frequency, read PEEK_I\n",
    "#     and PEEK_Q. Cheap, no memory, no transport, and exact at DC.\n",
    "#   * RECORDER. Stream to memory and analyse a time record. Needed for spectra,\n",
    "#     which the voltmeter cannot see.\n",
    "#\n",
    "#  ORDER MATTERS. The decimator holds a running total that assumes a fixed\n",
    "#  decimation factor, so changing R mid-stream leaves a meaningless partial sum\n",
    "#  in the pipeline. Always: disable -> write settings -> reset -> enable. That\n",
    "#  is what start() does. Retuning the local oscillator is exempt: it is one\n",
    "#  register write and is safe at any time.\n",
    "# =============================================================================\n",
    "class Acq(Bus):\n",
    "\n",
    "    def __init__(self):\n",
    "        Bus.__init__(self, 0x40600000)\n",
    "        self.dec = None\n",
    "\n",
    "    # ---- setup ----\n",
    "    def start(self, dec_log2, src=0):\n",
    "        \"\"\"Bring the chain up at a given decimation. Safe to call repeatedly.\"\"\"\n",
    "        self.w(0x00, 0)                 # disable while settings change\n",
    "        self.w(0x08, dec_log2)          # decimation R = 2**dec_log2\n",
    "        self.w(0x0C, src)               # 0 = IN1, 1 = IN2\n",
    "        self.w(0x00, 1 | 2)             # enable + soft reset (self-clearing)\n",
    "        self.w(0x00, 1)                 # running\n",
    "        self.dec = dec_log2\n",
    "\n",
    "    def stop(self):\n",
    "        self.w(0x00, 0)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # ---- tuning ----\n",
    "    def tune_word(self, finc):\n",
    "        \"\"\"Set the local oscillator by its raw 32-bit frequency word.\"\"\"\n",
    "        self.w(0x04, int(finc) & 0xFFFFFFFF)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def tune(self, f):\n",
    "        \"\"\"Set the local oscillator to the nearest word to f hertz.\"\"\"\n",
    "        self.tune_word(int(round(f / FS_CLK * (1 << 32))))\n",
    "\n",
    "    # ---- voltmeter ----\n",
    "    def measure(self, navg=None):\n",
    "        \"\"\"Average navg in-phase/quadrature pairs; return one complex number.\n",
    "\n",
    "        At DC both are steady, so this is coherent averaging: a real signal adds\n",
    "        linearly while noise averages down as the square root of navg.\"\"\"\n",
    "        navg = N_AVG if navg is None else navg\n",
    "        zi = 0.0\n",
    "        zq = 0.0\n",
    "        for _ in range(navg):\n",
    "            zi += self.rs(0x18)\n",
    "            zq += self.rs(0x1C)\n",
    "        return complex(zi / navg, zq / navg)\n",
    "\n",
    "    # ---- housekeeping ----\n",
    "    def f_out(self):\n",
    "        \"\"\"Output sample rate at the current decimation [Hz].\"\"\"\n",
    "        return FS_CLK / (1 << self.dec)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def window_hz(self):\n",
    "        \"\"\"Half-width of the CLEAN part of the receiver window [Hz].\n",
    "\n",
    "        The decimator rejects by better than 75 dB only over the middle fifth of\n",
    "        its output band, so the usable half-width is 0.1 x the output rate.\"\"\"\n",
    "        return 0.10 * self.f_out()\n",
    "\n",
    "    def build_id(self):\n",
    "        return self.r(0x48)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def status2(self):\n",
    "        return self.r(0x38)\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "acq = Acq()\n",
    "print('acquisition mapped. BUILD_ID = 0x%08X' % acq.build_id())"
   ]
  },
